    thinking: "**Analyzing the Problem**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the \"no-speech\" error in the React application using the Web Speech API. I\'m trying to break down the user\'s issue, looking at potential causes like microphone issues, environmental noise, or API limitations. I\'m moving toward a diagnosis.\n\n\n**Refining the Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on handling the `no-speech` error specifically within the `onerror` handler. My aim is to prevent abrupt recording halts or user confusion. It appears that the current implementation might be overreacting to silence. My current plan is to examine the behavior of `onerror` and `onend`, to restart the recognition service if necessary, while ignoring or logging the error.\n\n\n**Adjusting Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing targeted error handling. Specifically, I plan to handle the \'no-speech\' error within the `onerror` event. I\'m focusing on suppressing the console error message for \"no-speech\" since silence is expected. The goal is a smoother user experience where expected pauses are not flagged as errors. The `onend` event will be updated to handle this change. I\'m also planning to handle other potential error types such as `network`.\n\n\n**Implementing the Fix**\n\nI\'m now in the final stage of implementation. My focus is on refining the `onerror` event handler to specifically address and mitigate the \"not-allowed\" error. I\'m updating the `onerror` handler in `/src/App.tsx` using `edit_file`. The goal is to set the appropriate error message and stop recording when the microphone permission is denied, leading to a smooth recovery. I\'ve also checked to ensure the `onend` event is handled correctly.\n\n\n"
