    thinking: "**Focusing on Core Needs**\n\nI\'m zeroing in on the core request: implementing the audio upload feature. It\'s essential that no other functionality gets touched. The key constraint is to focus solely on integrating this new audio upload feature, without inadvertently altering any existing parts of the application or the user experience. This narrow scope should keep things manageable.\n\n\n**Evaluating Audio Integration**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the existing code to see where the audio upload fits. Analyzing `App.tsx`, I see the real-time speech and chunking logic. The Gemini API\'s audio support threw a curveball. Should I use Gemini\'s audio processing? I have to decide how to incorporate an uploaded audio file: transcript, whole file, or convert the base?\n\n\n**Integrating Audio Upload**\n\nI\'m leaning toward an \"Upload Audio\" button beside the record controls. The plan is to send the uploaded audio to Gemini. Based on the requirements, I need to either transcribe it or summarize it. Gemini can handle audio data as base64. The current focus is to refine the prompt for optimal results from Gemini, targeting either transcription or a summary with transcription, depending on the user\'s need for meeting minutes.\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Transcription**\n\nI\'ve decided to streamline the process: upload, transcribe, and summarize immediately. My plan is to create a new chunk containing both the transcription and summary generated by Gemini. The audio upload will trigger a prompt asking Gemini to \"Please transcribe this recording into a verbatim transcript, and briefly summarize the key points, decisions, and action items.\" I\'ve confirmed that Gemini Flash should easily handle a single audio upload, with a context limit of 9.5 hours. I\'m going to add a hidden file input behind an \"Upload Audio\" button.\n\n\n**Refining Gemini Prompt**\n\nI\'m now refining the prompt for Gemini to process the uploaded audio. I\'ve switched from asking for a verbatim transcript and summary separately to a single, more concise prompt: \"\350\253\213\350\201\275\351\200\231\346\256\265\351\214\204\351\237\263\357\274\214\344\270\246\346\217\220\344\276\233\357\274\232\\n1. \345\256\214\346\225\264\351\200\220\345\255\227\347\250\277\\n2. \351\207\215\351\273\236\347\270\275\347\265\220\". I\'ll be parsing the response or storing the entire response as is within the `chunk.text` and `chunk.summary` properties, keeping the flow consistent. I\'ll also add a hidden file input and the upload button.\n\n\n**Finalizing Chunk Structure**\n\nI\'ve determined the final chunk structure based on Gemini\'s capabilities. I\'ll pass the audio to Gemini for transcription and summarization in one go, avoiding separate prompts. The new `chunk.text` will hold the entire Gemini response in a specific format: a verbatim transcript followed by a summary. The prompt will be: \"\350\253\213\350\201\275\351\200\231\346\256\265\351\214\204\351\237\263\357\274\214\344\270\246\345\232\264\346\240\274\346\214\211\347\205\247\344\273\245\344\270\213\346\240\274\345\274\217\345\233\236\350\246\206\357\274\232\\n\\n\343\200\220\351\200\220\345\255\227\347\250\277\343\200\221\\n(\351\200\231\350\243\241\346\224\276\345\256\214\346\225\264\351\200\220\345\255\227\347\250\277)\\n\\n\343\200\220\351\207\215\351\273\236\347\270\275\347\265\220\343\200\221\" The `commitCurrentChunk` logic will then apply.\n\n\n**Revising Chunk Storage**\n\nI\'m now changing course regarding audio storage within the chunks. Considering potential audio file sizes, I\'ll store the base64 encoded audio data alongside the transcribed text and summary. The new chunk structure will include an `audioData` field, containing `mimeType` and `data`. The key is to manage the audio data effectively in the browser memory for potential future use. I\'ll pass the original audio data to Gemini during report generation if necessary.\n\n\n**Refining Chunk Updates**\n\nI\'m now integrating the uploaded audio with the chunk structure. I will add the audio details (filename, MIME type, and base64 data) to the new chunk. The text area will show \"\345\267\262\344\270\212\345\202\263\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\252\224: filename\", and the summary will display the generated summary from Gemini. When the user uploads an audio file, I will read it as base64, add a new chunk with the file details and a placeholder, and then call Gemini for a summary using the base64 data. Finally, I\'ll update the chunk with the received summary.\n\n\n**Constructing Final Report**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the final report generation. The goal is to incorporate both text and audio chunks effectively. Instead of a single prompt string, I will now use an array format for the Gemini API call. For each chunk, I\'ll format the `contextParts` based on whether it\'s text or audio, ensuring proper handling of audio filenames. I\'m building a prompt text to provide all the context to Gemini for the structured final output.\n\n\n**Generating the Final Report**\n\nI\'m now generating the final report with audio context. I\'ve switched to an array format for the Gemini API, structuring `contextParts` based on text or audio content within each chunk. This is extremely efficient because Gemini\'s model, specifically 1.5/2.5 Flash, natively processes audio during report generation. Now I just need to make sure the base64 conversion is efficient for this workflow.\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the UI for audio handling and refining the upload process. I\'m adding an upload icon, updating the chunk interface to include an optional audio object, and creating a hidden file input with a ref. I also need to create a `handleFileUpload` function to check file sizes and initiate the base64 conversion.\n\n\n**Optimizing Base64 Conversion**\n\nI\'m optimizing the base64 conversion for audio uploads to ensure efficient data handling within the application. I\'ll add a file size check to warn users if the file exceeds a certain limit (currently 15MB) and prevent potential API errors with a 400 Bad Request. I will convert the audio file to base64, add a new chunk to the state with the base64 data, and call Gemini with `inlineData` to process it, and update the chunk.\n\n\n**Integrating Audio Chunks**\n\nI\'m now integrating audio chunks into the report generation process, and I\'ve started using the array format for Gemini calls. I construct the `contextParts` array, including audio `inlineData` objects for the uploaded audio files, ensuring the format is consistent with the model\'s requirements. Gemini 1.5/2.5 Flash efficiently handles the audio content. The focus now is streamlining base64 conversion and report generation efficiency.\n\n\n**Finalizing Summarization Format**\n\nI\'m finalizing the format of the audio chunk summaries. I\'ve switched to a new API call structure, passing audio inline data to Gemini, along with a prompt. The prompt requests that Gemini generate a succinct summary with key points, decisions, and action items. I am testing the new format to ensure that Gemini\'s output is succinct and relevant.\n\n\n**Adding UI Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now adding the UI elements for audio uploads. I\'m adding an upload icon, updating the chunk interface to optionally include an audio object, and creating a hidden file input with a ref. I also need to create a `handleFileUpload` function. I\'m focusing on preserving the existing recording and manual chunking features, as requested.\n\n\n**Refining Upload Confirmation**\n\nI\'m now refining the upload confirmation feedback to the user and managing state updates more effectively. I\'m focusing on providing more immediate visual feedback after the audio upload. To minimize confusion, after an upload, the text area displays \"\345\267\262\344\270\212\345\202\263\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\252\224: filename\". Concurrently, I\'ve implemented logic to set the loading state. I then perform a base64 conversion, using this data in the Gemini call. I\'m focusing on updating the chunk.text with more details and improving error handling during file uploads.\n\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the error handling during audio uploads to provide a better user experience. Specifically, I\'m focusing on improved feedback when audio analysis fails. If the Gemini call fails, the chunk summary will display an error message in Chinese: \'\345\210\206\346\236\220\345\244\261\346\225\227\357\274\214\350\253\213\347\242\272\350\252\215\346\252\224\346\241\210\346\240\274\345\274\217\346\210\226\347\266\262\350\267\257\351\200\243\347\267\232\343\200\202\' to inform the user. I\'ve also incorporated the `isAnalyzing` loading state during base64 conversion.\n\n\n**Improving MIME Type Detection**\n\nI\'m now improving MIME type detection for uploaded audio files to handle edge cases and enhance browser compatibility. Specifically, I\'m addressing instances where `file.type` might be empty, especially on certain operating systems or browsers. I\'m implementing a fallback mechanism that checks file extensions (like `.m4a`, `.mp3`, `.wav`) to determine a suitable MIME type, and I added a default `audio/mpeg` for other extensions. I need to make sure this new logic works.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing Prompt Arrangement**\n\nI\'m now refining how the audio and text context is arranged within the Gemini API calls. I\'ve confirmed that interleaving `inlineData` (audio) and `text` parts in the `parts` array is supported by Gemini. I\'m now structuring the `parts` array as follows: audio parts are placed as `inlineData` objects, with the corresponding chunk text following. This approach improves the flow of context, enabling Gemini to generate more coherent final reports.\n\n\n**Constructing API Request**\n\nI\'m now building the Gemini API request payload for the final report generation. The `parts` array is being structured to clearly delineate text and audio chunks. The audio segments are included as `inlineData` objects, directly associated with their corresponding textual descriptions. The goal is to maximize contextual understanding for Gemini, enhancing the generated report\'s coherence and accuracy. I\'ve included a concise prompt that incorporates the overall context.\n\n\n**Integrating UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now integrating the updated UI elements into `App.tsx`, focusing on incorporating the audio upload feature seamlessly. I\'ve updated the import statements to include relevant icons for the upload button and loading indicators. I\'m adding `fileInputRef` to capture file uploads and refining the `handleFileUpload` function. Now, I\'m focusing on ensuring it handles the file size limit and base64 conversion correctly, incorporating the UI elements within the existing chunk structure.\n\n```typescript\n// ... (Previous imports and interfaces)\nimport { FileText, Loader2, Send, Copy, Check, Mic, Square, Scissors, AlertCircle, Upload } from \'lucide-react\';\n// ... (Other code)\n\ninterface Chunk {\n  id: number;\n  text: string;\n  summary?: string;\n  isAnalyzing: boolean;\n  audio?: {\n    fileName: string;\n    mimeType: string;\n    data: string;\n  };\n}\n\n// ... (Other code)\n\nconst recognitionRef = useRef<any>(null);\nconst isRecordingRef = useRef(false);\nconst currentChunkRef = useRef(\'\');\nconst fileInputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\n\nconst handleFileUpload = async (event: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {\n  const file = event.target.files?.[0];\n  if (!file) return;\n\n  if (fileInputRef.current) fileInputRef.current.value = \'\';\n\n  if (file.size > 15 * 1024 * 1024) {\n    setErrorMsg(\'\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\252\224\345\244\247\345\260\217\344\270\215\350\203\275\350\266\205\351\201\216 15MB\343\200\202\350\213\245\346\252\224\346\241\210\351\201\216\345\244\247\357\274\214\350\253\213\351\207\215\346\226\260\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\210\226\345\210\206\346\256\265\344\270\212\345\202\263\343\200\202\');\n    return;\n  }\n\n  setLoading(true);\n\n  // Determine MIME type\n  let mimeType = file.type;\n  if (!mimeType) {\n    const extension = file.name.split(\'.\').pop()?.toLowerCase();\n    switch (extension) {\n      case \'m4a\':\n        mimeType = \'audio/x-m4a\';\n        break;\n      case \'mp3\':\n        mimeType = \'audio/mpeg\';\n        break;\n      case \'wav\':\n        mimeType = \'audio/wav\';\n        break;\n      default:\n        mimeType = \'audio/mpeg\';\n    }\n  }\n\n  const reader = new FileReader();\n  reader.onload = async (e: ProgressEvent<FileReader>) => {\n    const base64Data = e.target?.result?.toString().split(\',\')[1];\n    if (!base64Data) {\n      setLoading(false);\n      setErrorMsg(\'\346\252\224\346\241\210\350\256\200\345\217\226\345\244\261\346\225\227\357\274\214\350\253\213\351\207\215\346\226\260\344\270\212\345\202\263\343\200\202\');\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const newChunk: Chunk = {\n      id: chunks.length + 1,\n      text: `\345\267\262\344\270\212\345\202\263\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\252\224: ${file.name}`,\n      isAnalyzing: true,\n      audio: {\n        fileName: file.name,\n        mimeType: mimeType,\n        data: base64Data,\n      },\n    };\n\n    setChunks((prevChunks) => [...prevChunks, newChunk]);\n    setCurrentChunkId(newChunk.id);\n    setCurrentChunkRef(newChunk.id.toString());\n    setTextareaValue(`\345\267\262\344\270\212\345\202\263\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\252\224: ${file.name}`);\n\n    try {\n      const response = await fetch(\'/api/gemini\', {\n        method: \'POST\',\n        headers: {\n          \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\',\n        },\n        body: JSON.stringify({\n          parts: [\n            {\n              text: \'\350\253\213\350\201\275\351\200\231\346\256\265\351\214\204\351\237\263\357\274\214\344\270\246\346\217\220\344\276\233\357\274\232\\n1. \345\256\214\346\225\264\351\200\220\345\255\227\347\250\277\\n2.