  planner_response {
    response: "\346\210\221\345\267\262\347\266\223\347\202\272\346\202\250\350\247\243\351\231\244\344\272\206 15MB \347\232\204\344\270\212\345\202\263\351\231\220\345\210\266\357\274\214\344\270\246\345\260\216\345\205\245\344\272\206**\343\200\214\346\231\272\346\205\247\345\236\213\351\237\263\346\252\224\345\210\206\346\256\265\350\231\225\347\220\206\346\212\200\350\241\223\343\200\215**\343\200\202\n\n### \351\200\231\346\254\241\345\215\207\347\264\232\347\232\204\346\240\270\345\277\203\351\202\217\350\274\257\357\274\232\n1. **\350\247\243\351\231\244\347\241\254\346\200\247\351\231\220\345\210\266**\357\274\232\347\247\273\351\231\244\345\216\237\346\234\254\347\232\204 15MB \346\252\242\346\237\245\357\274\214\347\217\276\345\234\250\346\202\250\345\217\257\344\273\245\344\270\212\345\202\263\346\233\264\345\244\247\347\232\204\346\234\203\350\255\260\351\214\204\351\237\263\346\252\224\343\200\202\n2. **\350\207\252\345\213\225\351\231\215\351\240\273\350\210\207\345\226\256\350\201\262\351\201\223\350\231\225\347\220\206**\357\274\232\347\202\272\344\272\206\347\242\272\344\277\235\345\244\247\345\236\213\346\252\224\346\241\210\350\203\275\350\242\253 AI \345\256\214\346\225\264\350\256\200\345\217\226\357\274\214\347\263\273\347\265\261\345\234\250\344\270\212\345\202\263\346\231\202\346\234\203\350\207\252\345\213\225\345\260\207\351\237\263\346\252\224\350\275\211\347\202\272 **16kHz \345\226\256\350\201\262\351\201\223 (Mono)**\343\200\202\351\200\231\350\203\275\345\244\247\345\271\205\345\243\223\347\270\256\346\252\224\346\241\210\351\253\224\347\251\215\357\274\214\345\220\214\346\231\202\344\277\235\347\225\231\346\270\205\346\231\260\347\232\204\350\252\236\351\237\263\350\263\207\350\250\212\357\274\214\350\256\223 AI \346\233\264\345\256\271\346\230\223\350\231\225\347\220\206\343\200\202\n3. **\346\231\202\351\225\267\350\207\252\345\213\225\345\210\207\347\211\207 (Chunking)**\357\274\232\345\246\202\346\236\234\351\237\263\346\252\224\351\201\216\351\225\267\357\274\210\344\276\213\345\246\202\350\266\205\351\201\216 10 \345\210\206\351\220\230\357\274\211\357\274\214\347\263\273\347\265\261\346\234\203\350\207\252\345\213\225\345\210\251\347\224\250\347\200\217\350\246\275\345\231\250\347\232\204 Web Audio API \345\260\207\345\205\266\345\210\207\345\210\206\347\202\272\346\225\270\345\200\213 10 \345\210\206\351\220\230\347\232\204\345\260\217\346\256\265\350\220\275\343\200\202\n   * \346\257\217\345\200\213\346\256\265\350\220\275\346\234\203\347\215\250\347\253\213\347\224\242\347\224\237\344\270\200\345\200\213\343\200\214\345\210\206\346\256\265\345\210\206\346\236\220\343\200\215\343\200\202\n   * \351\200\231\346\250\243\345\201\232\350\203\275\351\201\277\345\205\215\345\226\256\346\254\241 API \350\253\213\346\261\202\351\201\216\345\244\247\345\260\216\350\207\264\345\244\261\346\225\227\357\274\214\345\220\214\346\231\202\347\242\272\344\277\235\351\225\267\351\201\224 1\343\200\2012 \345\260\217\346\231\202\347\232\204\346\234\203\350\255\260\344\271\237\350\203\275\350\242\253**\351\200\220\346\256\265\347\262\276\346\272\226\345\210\206\346\236\220**\343\200\202\n4. **\345\256\214\346\225\264\346\225\264\345\220\210**\357\274\232\346\234\200\345\276\214\347\224\237\346\210\220\345\240\261\345\221\212\346\231\202\357\274\214AI \346\234\203\346\225\264\345\220\210\346\211\200\346\234\211\345\210\207\347\211\207\347\232\204\345\210\206\346\236\220\347\265\220\346\236\234\357\274\214\347\224\242\345\207\272\346\234\200\345\256\214\346\225\264\347\232\204\346\234\203\350\255\260\347\264\200\351\214\204\343\200\202\n\n\350\253\213\347\234\213\344\270\213\346\226\271\347\232\204\347\250\213\345\274\217\347\242\274\346\233\264\346\226\260\357\274\232\n\n"
    thinking: "**Addressing File Size Limits**\n\nI\'m currently focused on removing the 15MB file size restriction for audio uploads. The existing code uses a hardcoded check, which needs immediate revision. My plan is to remove the if statement and then ensure the system can handle the analysis of much larger audio files. I\'ll need to research the ramifications of increasing the size limit, and consider performance implications.\n\n\n**Refining File Handling**\n\nI\'ve realized that simply removing the size limit isn\'t sufficient given browser and API constraints. I\'m now exploring client-side chunking as a viable solution. This involves splitting audio files into smaller segments before analysis, sending each chunk for summary, and then combining the results. The challenge is ensuring seamless audio splitting, and the potential impact of this approach on the summary accuracy of Gemini 3.1 Flash Preview.\n\n\n**Considering Chunking Strategies**\n\nI\'m now refining the chunking implementation, recognizing the need to balance simplicity with robustness. I\'m leaning toward using the Web Audio API for audio slicing, which offers a robust method. I\'m investigating how best to re-encode chunks, or slice the raw blob for simpler formats while avoiding header corruption, to ensure proper processing by Gemini.\n\n\n**Exploring API Request Limits**\n\nI\'m now focusing on API request limits with Gemini 3.1 Flash Preview. Encoding audio in the browser seems complex without external libraries, so I\'m investigating sending the entire file directly to the API, and notifying the user. A 50MB audio file becomes ~67MB in base64, potentially exceeding the standard 20MB limit. I\'m considering using simple \"blob slicing\" and hoping Gemini can handle partial headers, or using Web Audio API to slice by time. My goal is full analysis of very large audio files, and chunking into 10-minute segments seems a very reliable way to ensure this. I\'ve now found that `OfflineAudioContext` can decode the file, a solid first step.\n\n\n**Implementing WAV Chunking**\n\nI\'m now implementing WAV chunking, focusing on the steps to overcome the file size constraints. First, I\'ll remove the existing 15MB limit and then decode the audio data using `AudioContext`, since it has the potential for failure if it\'s too large, but shouldn\'t fail with typical files. My plan is to split audio files into roughly 10-minute segments and encode each chunk as a WAV file before sending it to Gemini. I will then send each chunk and add them as segments in the UI. I have to create a WAV header for this. The next step is checking the current code, and writing the helper function.\n\n\n**Refining Chunk Encoding**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation of audio chunking, specifically focusing on encoding the segments as WAV files. I\'m building a helper function, starting with the audioBufferToWav logic. This involves generating the WAV header, including RIFF and WAVE chunk identifiers, file length, and PCM encoding details. My current focus is to ensure the generated WAV headers are valid and can be properly interpreted by the API. I\'m writing the code now to generate the correct headers and file structure for each audio chunk.\n\n\n**Validating WAV Header Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the WAV chunk encoding, working to ensure generated headers are valid. I\'m focusing on the helper function and using the `audioBufferToWav` logic. I\'m writing the code that creates the WAV header, including the necessary RIFF, WAVE chunk identifiers, file length, and PCM encoding information. I\'m making sure that the generated header works properly with the API by double-checking the code. I am now working on the header validation, and will test after I have validation implemented.\n\n\n"
