  planner_response {
    response: "\351\200\231\346\230\257\344\270\200\345\200\213\351\235\236\345\270\270\347\266\223\345\205\270\347\232\204\347\200\217\350\246\275\345\231\250\350\250\230\346\206\266\351\253\224\351\231\220\345\210\266\345\225\217\351\241\214\343\200\202\347\225\266\346\210\221\345\200\221\344\275\277\347\224\250 `decodeAudioData` \346\231\202\357\274\214\347\200\217\350\246\275\345\231\250\346\234\203\345\260\207\345\243\223\347\270\256\347\232\204\351\237\263\346\252\224\357\274\210\345\246\202 MP3\357\274\211\351\202\204\345\216\237\346\210\220**\345\216\237\345\247\213\347\232\204 PCM \346\225\270\346\223\232**\343\200\202\344\270\200\345\200\213 1 \345\260\217\346\231\202\347\232\204 MP3 \345\217\257\350\203\275\345\217\252\346\234\211 50MB\357\274\214\344\275\206\350\247\243\347\242\274\346\210\220 PCM \345\276\214\346\234\203\347\236\254\351\226\223\350\206\250\350\204\271\345\210\260 **600MB ~ 1GB**\357\274\214\351\200\231\346\234\203\347\233\264\346\216\245\345\260\216\350\207\264\347\200\217\350\246\275\345\231\250\345\210\206\351\240\201\345\264\251\346\275\260\343\200\202\n\n\347\202\272\344\272\206\345\276\271\345\272\225\350\247\243\346\261\272\351\200\231\345\200\213\345\225\217\351\241\214\357\274\214\346\210\221\351\207\215\346\226\260\350\250\255\350\250\210\344\272\206**\343\200\214\346\231\272\346\205\247\345\236\213\351\233\231\350\273\214\344\270\212\345\202\263\346\251\237\345\210\266\343\200\215**\357\274\232\n\n### 1. \351\233\231\350\273\214\350\231\225\347\220\206\351\202\217\350\274\257\n*   **\345\260\217\346\226\274 25MB \347\232\204\346\252\224\346\241\210\357\274\210\347\233\264\346\216\245\345\202\263\351\200\201\357\274\211**\357\274\232\344\270\215\345\206\215\351\200\262\350\241\214\344\273\273\344\275\225\350\247\243\347\242\274\350\231\225\347\220\206\343\200\202\347\233\264\346\216\245\345\260\207\345\216\237\345\247\213\347\232\204 MP3/M4A/WAV \350\275\211\347\202\272 Base64 \345\202\263\347\265\246 Gemini\343\200\202\351\200\231\350\203\275**\345\256\214\345\205\250\351\201\277\345\205\215\350\247\243\347\242\274\347\224\242\347\224\237\347\232\204\350\250\230\346\206\266\351\253\224\345\243\223\345\212\233**\357\274\214\351\200\237\345\272\246\346\234\200\345\277\253\344\270\224\346\234\200\347\251\251\345\256\232\343\200\202\n*   **\345\244\247\346\226\274 25MB \347\232\204\346\252\224\346\241\210\357\274\210\345\256\211\345\205\250\345\210\206\346\256\265\350\247\243\347\242\274\357\274\211**\357\274\232\n    *   \346\210\221\345\200\221\344\276\235\347\204\266\351\234\200\350\246\201\351\231\215\351\240\273\345\243\223\347\270\256\357\274\214\344\275\206\346\210\221\345\204\252\345\214\226\344\272\206\350\231\225\347\220\206\346\265\201\347\250\213\357\274\214\347\242\272\344\277\235\345\234\250\350\247\243\347\242\274\345\244\247\345\236\213\346\252\224\346\241\210\346\231\202\357\274\214\345\204\230\345\217\257\350\203\275\351\207\213\346\224\276\344\270\215\345\277\205\350\246\201\347\232\204\346\232\253\345\255\230\350\250\230\346\206\266\351\253\224\343\200\202\n    *   \345\204\252\345\214\226\344\272\206 `audioBufferToWav` \347\232\204\346\225\210\350\203\275\357\274\214\346\270\233\345\260\221\346\225\270\346\223\232\350\244\207\350\243\275\343\200\202\n\n### 2. \346\225\210\350\203\275\345\204\252\345\214\226\n*   **\347\247\273\351\231\244\351\207\215\350\244\207\347\267\251\350\241\235\345\215\200**\357\274\232\347\242\272\344\277\235\345\234\250\350\247\243\347\242\274\345\276\214\347\253\213\345\215\263\351\207\213\346\224\276\345\216\237\345\247\213\347\232\204 `ArrayBuffer`\343\200\202\n*   **\351\235\236\345\220\214\346\255\245 Base64 \350\275\211\346\217\233**\357\274\232\344\275\277\347\224\250 `FileReader` \350\231\225\347\220\206 Blob\357\274\214\351\200\231\346\230\257\347\233\256\345\211\215\347\200\217\350\246\275\345\231\250\350\231\225\347\220\206\345\244\247\345\236\213\346\225\270\346\223\232\346\234\200\347\251\251\345\256\232\347\232\204\346\226\271\345\274\217\343\200\202\n\n\344\273\245\344\270\213\346\230\257\344\277\256\345\276\251\345\276\214\347\232\204\347\250\213\345\274\217\347\242\274\357\274\232\n\n"
    thinking: "**Addressing the Crashes**\n\nI\'m still grappling with the crashing issue during large audio file uploads. The initial focus on Base64 optimization and downsampling hasn\'t fully resolved it. Now, I\'m diving deeper into the `audioCtx.decodeAudioData(arrayBuffer)` function, as the crash seems to be occurring there during the decoding process with larger file sizes. I\'m exploring potential memory limitations and looking at more efficient audio decoding strategies.\n\n\n**Refining File Handling**\n\nI\'ve realized the bottleneck is the massive PCM data from decoding entire audio files. Decoding a 1-hour file can result in hundreds of MB or even GB in memory, exceeding browser limits, especially on mobile. The standard Web Audio API\'s `decodeAudioData` doesn\'t support streaming, so I\'m investigating alternative approaches to handle large files. Gemini 1.5 Flash could work, but user constraints are preventing that currently. Direct Gemini uploads are also restricted to 20MB. I\'m now exploring slicing the Blob instead of the decoded data.\n\n\n**Evaluating File Strategies**\n\nI\'m now thinking about strategies based on file size and format. For smaller files, I could skip client-side decoding altogether and let Gemini handle the compressed data directly. Larger files, especially those greater than 20MB, present a challenge since direct Gemini uploads have a limit, though the `@google/genai` library might permit more. I\'m exploring server-side slicing and a combination of client-side and server-side operations depending on file properties.\n\n\n**Investigating AudioDecoder**\n\nI\'ve hit a potential breakthrough! While earlier chunking attempts failed, I\'ve rediscovered the WebCodecs API\'s `AudioDecoder`. This frame-by-frame decoding seems ideal for memory efficiency. I\'m now exploring MP3/M4A demuxing complexity within it, but initial impressions are promising for significantly reduced memory footprint. Modern Chrome support is there, too!\n\n\n**Simplifying Strategy**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a \"Safety First\" strategy: for smaller files (under 30MB), I\'ll try direct Base64 upload to Gemini. Larger files will be sliced. I\'m exploring using a hidden `<audio>` element with `captureStream()` and `MediaRecorder` to create manageable segments, but real-time recording is too slow.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Exploring Gemini Capabilities**\n\nI\'ve realized complete analysis is the main goal, and Gemini 1.5/3 Flash can handle longer audio, given file size constraints. While the direct inlineData limit is generous, 20MB is still the safe bet. So, even simpler: for reasonable size files, it might be possible to let Gemini process it directly, perhaps without even having to transcode, assuming this is an option!\n\n\n**Simplifying Audio Handling**\n\nI\'m now thinking I can avoid the memory-intensive `decodeAudioData` step altogether for files under 20MB by sending the Base64 encoded MP3 directly. This streamlines the process and avoids crashes from excessive PCM data. For larger files, the downsampling and chopping strategy will still be needed, but for the vast majority of files, direct sending is feasible. My focus is now on ensuring efficient and stable handling for different file sizes to optimize the user experience.\n\n\n**Optimizing Data Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on memory management during audio processing. I\'m exploring the WebCodecs API as a potential solution, though its implementation seems complex. Even with a new downsampling \"trick\", the root cause may be holding the `arrayBuffer` while decoding data. I\'m experimenting with nulling out the `arrayBuffer` sooner to free up memory. Using `Blob` and `URL.createObjectURL` is still insufficient, since `decodeAudioData` requires an `ArrayBuffer`. I\'m now thinking about using web workers.\n\n\n**Revising the Approach**\n\nI\'ve realized I can streamline things further. Since Gemini 3 Flash can handle up to 50MB inline, I\'ll prioritize direct Base64 uploads without decoding for files under 40MB. This eliminates the risk of decoding crashes. I\'m checking existing code to determine how best to approach that. Files exceeding 40MB will be considered for chunking or file size notification, but I want to avoid these options if possible.\n\n\n**Refining Direct Sending**\n\nI\'ve decided to refine the \"Smart Strategy\". I realized that decoding is the major source of crashes, so I\'ll prioritize skipping that step. Now, I\'m checking file format and size. If a file is under 20MB and a common audio format, I\'ll send it directly without decoding. For larger files, I\'ll attempt downsampling with more robust memory handling using `OfflineAudioContext`, and ensure data does not persist in the buffer during decoding by using a `Response` object to handle the `ArrayBuffer`.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Decoding Reduction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on reducing memory footprint during audio processing. While the direct upload approach up to 25MB will handle the majority of cases, I\'m examining how to minimize the memory load for larger files. The decoding process remains the bottleneck, so I am going to see if I can reduce the memory footprint by ensuring that `arrayBuffer` is detached/freed after `decodeAudioData`. I\'m now testing a direct upload option for the typical cases where it will be applicable to cover the primary use cases.\n\n\n**Implementing Direct Upload**\n\nI\'m now implementing the \"Direct MP3/M4A/WAV Upload\" strategy for files under 30MB, which I believe will be a major crash prevention solution. My first step is using `FileReader` to get the Base64 data without decoding. If the file is 30MB or larger, I\'ll attempt downsampling with a memory warning. I\'m also exploring using an `audio` element for playback at a slower rate and recording for larger files, aiming for a more browser-native and safer approach.\n\n\n**Refining Upload Strategy**\n\nI\'m now revising my approach. Direct Base64 upload for smaller files remains key. I\'m focusing on handling larger audio more efficiently. I\'ll utilize a hidden `<audio>` element with `captureStream()` and `MediaRecorder` to create manageable segments, drastically reducing decode operations. I\'m aiming for a \"playback rate\" trick, using `audio.playbackRate = 16` to drastically speed up processing.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
