    thinking: "**Addressing the Unexpected Error**\n\nI\'ve just added detailed loading status updates and timeout mechanisms for FFmpeg, hoping to tackle the \"unexpected error\" the user reported. I\'ve also implemented a \"Compatibility Mode\" using Blob Slicing as a fallback for large files, just in case FFmpeg fails. I am still looking into the error, and hope that these improvements address it.\n\n\n**Refining the Fallback**\n\nI\'ve added a UI toggle for \"Compatibility Mode\" and verified the build and linting. I\'m focusing on the \"always showing load failure\" issue, and the \"300MB large file\" the user mentioned. The slicing implementation is in place, but I realized the time estimate in the UI text is based on a rough 5MB chunk assumption and is inaccurate. I need to revisit the time estimate logic in `processFileBySlicing` to calculate a more precise estimate.\n\n\n**Estimating Byte-Based Duration**\n\nI\'ve refined the time estimation for compatibility mode, realizing it\'s byte-based. For a 300MB file, I\'ve adjusted the estimate to be clearer, and the math now works out. I\'ve confirmed that the `SYSTEM_PROMPT` is correctly used in `generateFinalReport`. I also checked the last response and it seemed complete, so I\'m not sure what to change here.\n\n\n**Verifying Mode Handling**\n\nI\'m verifying the compatibility mode logic, and checking `metadata.json` for any necessary updates. I\'ve confirmed `useCompatibilityMode` in `App.tsx` handles large files (greater than 20MB, except for .m4a files) and implements the intended fallback when FFmpeg is unavailable. I\'ve re-examined the code, confirming status updates are set correctly in `processFileBySlicing` and noting that a 15MB slice size is reasonable. I\'ve confirmed that the audio and metadata file load correctly.\n\n\n"
