  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Rate Limits**\n\nI\'m seeing a continued \"RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED\" error, despite earlier attempts at implementing retry logic and increasing delays between chunk submissions. It seems the rate limit or quota is still being hit. I\'m taking a closer look at the exact rate limits for this API and how the current request patterns align, to pinpoint the bottleneck.\n\n\n**Refining Request Strategy**\n\nI\'ve been examining the parallel processing. It turns out I made a crucial oversight: the `analyzeChunk` function wasn\'t being awaited within the processing loop. This meant many requests could be active concurrently, potentially overwhelming the rate limits, especially for free tier users. Now, I\'m refactoring the code to process chunks sequentially, ensuring that each analysis completes before the next one begins. This should give a better handle on the request rate.\n\n\n**Revising Rate Limiting**\n\nI\'m thinking the backoff strategy needs a rethink, perhaps a more aggressive initial delay or an increased backoff period is required. I need to verify that `gemini-2.5-flash`\'s limits are the ones I should be following, too, as the suggested models were different. Reviewing the code, I realized I failed to `await` the `analyzeChunk` function in the processing loop, which explains the continued resource exhaustion. Now I will modify the loop to `await` the function call and introduce an additional delay.\n\n\n**Selecting Audio Model**\n\nI\'ve realized I was using the wrong Gemini model. My focus now is on `gemini-2.5-flash-native-audio-preview-12-2025` for optimal audio processing, as the system prompt suggests. I have reviewed the system prompt again, confirming the model for native audio handling. The implementation will now use this model to try for improved performance. My prior model selection was based on a misunderstanding of the versioning.\n\n\n"
